    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 213,
        "text": "Hi everyone, welcome to the Science Café. Tonight we're talking about whether scientists should share their early findings online. I'm Maya, and with me is Dr. Chen, a biologist. Dr. Chen, thanks for coming.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "d08730711491ba079f025bee87b61f2fd0685e63245ac5b7951f1080b716e48a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 224,
        "sourceEnd": 259,
        "text": "Thanks, Maya. It's good to be here.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "96006c3896416607bed535f5be5ad6a70893ac73cab3d9e93750524e401db1c5",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 266,
        "sourceEnd": 416,
        "text": "So, let's start with a simple question. Do you think it's a good idea for researchers to post their results before other scientists have checked them?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "1c4562af4942a6af030bd8c7e07aafd5465bb46d03e0f7faf596290e1b5a41a0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 427,
        "sourceEnd": 622,
        "text": "That's a big question. I think it depends on what you mean by 'results'. If you mean early ideas, then yes, sharing can help. But if you mean final conclusions, then no—those need checking first.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "887b1b26449ed5782ea449c518cf0ea5afb1210baf63de4e4b44f4f5bd8a627f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 629,
        "sourceEnd": 713,
        "text": "I see. Some people say that sharing early ideas speeds up science. What's your view?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "53e2644d6e7702a9cae09ed33799e3070514ac736882ea17b40fae0bcc3aa049",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 724,
        "sourceEnd": 929,
        "text": "I agree, but only if the sharing is done carefully. For example, last year my team posted some early data online. Another group saw it and pointed out a mistake in our method. That saved us months of work.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "712fec71e29eb2bc3ba70c30340a77f15aaec0abc939953b67b636f5c185db20",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 936,
        "sourceEnd": 1018,
        "text": "That's interesting. But what about the public? Could early sharing confuse people?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "48f6d04e7d22a2819d27fb8289a8dfd2b6f2cc8c793f3104c99a754ab3888252",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1029,
        "sourceEnd": 1255,
        "text": "Yes, that's a real risk. If people see a headline like 'New drug cures cancer' before the research is complete, they might get the wrong idea. That's why I think scientists should explain clearly that the work is not finished.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "26b14884353a04ac80d0919eaa8f73d965aed331fee432771fb228348020231d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1262,
        "sourceEnd": 1305,
        "text": "So you're saying that communication is key.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "af77b22875faf17d581c4469c162db23097f5dfa6e82ef4d1eb6e897bacaa090",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1316,
        "sourceEnd": 1462,
        "text": "Exactly. And I'd add that we need to be honest about uncertainty. Science is not about having all the answers; it's about asking better questions.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "cb54958c19cac3f72b3c79939840ae87afd45bb6f675828ce003a69cebb0d012",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1469,
        "sourceEnd": 1591,
        "text": "Let's move on. Some listeners have asked whether scientists should respond to comments on social media. What do you think?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "e2e542a50f2c9eca21af9db5f748a4890aa3ac1dd426c67f389896fabb27e6b0",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1602,
        "sourceEnd": 1817,
        "text": "I think they should, but not always. If a comment is rude, it's better to ignore it. But if it's a serious question, then answering can build trust. However, it takes time, and many scientists are already very busy.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "f63f8be800b5591ed945683bf57658768e36c9a124756710377c007049429406",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 1824,
        "sourceEnd": 1927,
        "text": "One listener, Tom, asks: 'Why don't scientists just wait until the research is perfect before sharing?'",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "80009a91cccc0b14b0970e9c7dbffaae9c78eb2e517ba9345860d7439422339b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 1938,
        "sourceEnd": 2112,
        "text": "That's a fair question. The problem is that research is never perfect. Waiting for perfection means waiting forever. So we share early, but we label it clearly as early work.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "64e330b85e3f249ce577c0eeeea1d252d80d1e3da3b1e58ba76e22b02163e1ac",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2119,
        "sourceEnd": 2214,
        "text": "OK. And finally, what advice would you give to young scientists who want to communicate better?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "5d7de96f373af41b3e5e814b5bfcdb2f95d5142b88a30ba78f3f1c6f3d9bbe7b",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 2225,
        "sourceEnd": 2400,
        "text": "I'd say: know your audience. If you're talking to the public, avoid jargon. Use simple words. And don't be afraid to say 'I don't know'. That's part of being a good scientist.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "673a157a0834c0e399667af71619727233e553ddaefac83c6734507ac829a49f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "Maya",
        "sourceStart": 2407,
        "sourceEnd": 2441,
        "text": "Great advice. Thank you, Dr. Chen.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-NatashaNeural",
        "textHash": "2269fc61133b87914fd14885fc3fa6e5ddcb6c20ae25ba75ef7a734495772ef3",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "dr chen",
        "speaker": "Dr. Chen",
        "sourceStart": 2452,
        "sourceEnd": 2462,
        "text": "Thank you.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-AU-WilliamNeural",
        "textHash": "8569efc4ed5a6273140c2b6ec0ab8d99f4398210808fbb7c0cbbae4fbcc21350",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      }
    ]
  },
